  <data key="d37">Increased drone and missile strikes on ships and near-port approaches force temporary port closures and route diversions, concentrating traffic through fewer passages and producing backlogs at remaining chokepoints.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">shipping_chokepoints</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::drone_missile_strike_rat" target="us_china_tech_trade::component_avail" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising rates of attacks on ships and port facilities cause cargo damage, detention for inspections, insurance-driven rerouting and port closures that directly reduce on‑hand and in‑transit quantities of critical components bound for US–China tech supply chains.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_trade</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::drone_missile_strike_rat" target="us_iran_standoff::maritime_incident_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">High-intensity use of drones and missiles in Ukraine accelerates secondary arms flows and tactical diffusion to external actors and proxies, enabling more capable and frequent attacks/interdictions against ships in the US–Iran maritime corridor.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_iran_standoff</data>
</edge>
